  "question": {
    "text": "Formation of which complex, among the following, is not a confirmatory test of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> ions"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Lead sulphate",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Lead nitrate",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "Lead chromate",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Lead iodide",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>On adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>, white precipitate of lead sulphate, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PbSO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is formed.&nbsp; By&nbsp;adding potassium iodide solution to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math> - Yellow precipitate of lead iodide is formed. The ppt dissolves in boiling water and on cooling recrystallises.&nbsp;</p><p>By&nbsp;adding potassium chromate solution to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Pb</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>&ndash;Yellow precipitate of lead chromate is formed. But lead nitrate is a water soluble compound.</p></blockquote>"
  }
